steps.detail:
If you suspect a heart attack, treat it as one (Red Cross). Do not wait to see if the pain goes away, and do not let the person talk you out of getting help (MedlinePlus).

Common signs (Red Cross, MedlinePlus, AHA 2024):
- chest pain, pressure, squeezing, fullness, tightness or heaviness, usually in the centre of the chest, lasting more than 3 to 5 minutes, or going away and coming back;
- pain or discomfort spreading to the arms (often the left), shoulder, neck, jaw, back or stomach;
- shortness of breath or trouble breathing;
- cold sweat, nausea or vomiting, indigestion, lightheadedness or dizziness;
- pale, grey or bluish skin; a feeling of anxiety or that something terrible is happening;
- numbness, aching or tingling in an arm.

Less typical signs: women, older adults and people with diabetes more often have unusual or milder symptoms (AHA 2024, MedlinePlus, Red Cross). They may have shortness of breath, pain in the back, neck, jaw or stomach, sweating, nausea, lightheadedness, or extreme tiredness or weakness, with little or no chest pain. In older adults, a change in mental state can be the sign (MedlinePlus). Women may have symptoms for hours, days or weeks before (Red Cross). Someone who has had a heart attack before may have different symptoms the second time (Red Cross).

Most people who die of a heart attack die within the first 2 hours of symptoms (Red Cross).
